1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,839
(Kamran) Kamran Razmavar presents another strange argument, and Jesus did NOT meet Armenians, and in the Bible, there are NO Armenians at all, so ancient Armenia is fabricated.

2
00:00:09,199 --> 00:00:12,279
(Naran) But the existence of countries and peoples is NOT established this way.

3
00:00:12,580 --> 00:00:16,359
(Naran) And now let's debunk this myth about Greater Armenia, which allegedly included Jerusalem.

4
00:00:16,920 --> 00:00:21,500
(Other Speaker) According to Wikipedia, Greater Armenia therefore did not exist in the fourth century, so our era is approximately the fifth.

5
00:00:21,760 --> 00:00:28,019
(Naran) And somewhere in the middle of these 80 years, Jesus Christ was born in Bethlehem, by the way, which is near Jerusalem.

6
00:00:28,019 --> 00:00:36,899
(Kamran) Strangely enough, Jesus did NOT meet a single Armenian. There is NOTHING Armenian in the Bible. There is NO great Armenia, there are dozens of state nationalities and NO ethnocentrism.

7
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:41,960
(Kamran) But there are NO Armenians, NO Armenia, and there is NO need to insert it there, it is written 'Mountains of Ararat'. This is a geographical term.

8
00:00:42,259 --> 00:00:50,200
(Naran) The Gospels are NOT a list of all the peoples of the Near East. The absence of a people in the story of Christ's life does NOT mean their absence in history.

9
00:00:50,200 --> 00:00:55,359
(Naran) Moreover, the name Armenia is recorded approximately 5 centuries before the birth of Jesus.

10
00:00:55,820 --> 00:01:00,799
(Naran) Around 520 BC, Darius the First left the Behistun inscription.

11
00:01:01,179 --> 00:01:05,500
(Other Speaker) In the Dri-nepersis text, the country is called 'Armina of the inhabitant of Armenia'.

12
00:01:05,500 --> 00:01:12,200
(Naran) The Encyclopedia Iranica translates the latter as Armenian. Now to Kamran's claim that there is Ararat in the Bible, but there are NO Armenians.

13
00:01:13,060 --> 00:01:16,700
(Naran) Even this is NOT true if we talk about Biblical translations in general.

14
00:01:16,700 --> 00:01:29,719
(Other Speaker) In the classic English translation of the King James Bible, in 2 Kings 19:37 and in the book of Isaiah 37:38, the expression land of Armenia is directly used.

15
00:01:30,219 --> 00:01:34,120
(Naran) In modern translations, in these same places, the land of Ararat is more often used.

16
00:01:34,299 --> 00:01:36,700
(Naran) And this shows the main problem with Kamran's argument.

17
00:01:37,599 --> 00:01:43,239
(Naran) Different linguistic and translation traditions can convey ancient geographical names differently.

18
00:01:43,239 --> 00:01:54,680
(Naran) This is also seen in the Behistun inscription. Ancient Persian versions use the name Armina, while in Velosko-Uras, which means the old Mesopotamian name 'Urartu' that therefore did not exist earlier in the same region.

19
00:01:54,939 --> 00:02:00,159
(Other Speaker) There are independent Greek testimonies. Herodotus from the fifth century BC mentions Armenians.

20
00:02:00,359 --> 00:02:05,659
(Other Speaker) Xenophon in the early 4th century BC repeatedly writes about Armenia and Armenians.

21
00:02:05,859 --> 00:02:10,960
(Naran) Therefore, Kamran's question of why Jesus did NOT meet Armenians historically proves nothing.

22
00:02:10,960 --> 00:02:16,740
(Naran) Persian and Greek sources record Armenia and Armenians a century before 'the birth of Christ'.

23
00:02:16,740 --> 00:02:27,879
(Naran) And the attempt to prove the non-existence of Armenia with the word 'Ararat' collapses on the sources themselves. Kamran simply mixes different linguistic traditions and takes the difference in names for the absence of a country.

24
00:02:25,879 --> 00:02:28,039
(Other Speaker) we will call them erreicht assignates,

